WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.970
Contrast-Consistent Search, or CCS,

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:09.601
is an unsupervised probe that reads a language
model's sense of truth from its activations.

00:00:09.681 --> 00:00:12.225
But what does CCS actually optimize?

00:00:12.305 --> 00:00:17.835
This paper explains its target and derives a new
loss, Midpoint-Displacement,

00:00:17.915 --> 00:00:20.240
that matches CCS and can beat it.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:26.967
CCS recovers a direction in a model's activations
encoding whether a statement is true or false,

00:00:27.047 --> 00:00:32.322
using no labels, only the constraint that a
statement and its negation disagree.

00:00:32.402 --> 00:00:36.205
Yet nobody had pinned down what its loss really optimizes,

00:00:36.285 --> 00:00:38.280
or whether that target is best.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.115
Safely deploying capable models means catching
confident falsehoods,

00:00:43.195 --> 00:00:47.583
and probes that read a model's own truth
representation could help,

00:00:47.663 --> 00:00:49.650
but only if we understand them.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:54.718
CCS was explained through clustering activations
and learning probabilities.

00:00:54.798 --> 00:01:00.320
The authors argue both pictures mislead, motivating
a cleaner account of its target.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.326
The paper makes three contributions.

00:01:04.406 --> 00:01:08.031
First,

00:01:08.111 --> 00:01:11.737
it corrects two misconceptions: CCS classifies
using only the displacement between a statement and its negation,

00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:15.443
needing no separating hyperplane,

00:01:15.523 --> 00:01:19.149
and succeeds even when probabilities cluster near
one half.

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:22.854
Second, it derives a new Midpoint-Displacement loss.

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:26.560
Third, a comparison shows it proxies, and can beat, CCS.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.338
CCS is described by two statistics along the probe
direction: sigma-d-squared,

00:01:32.418 --> 00:01:37.605
how far a statement and its negation are pushed
apart, and sigma-m-squared,

00:01:37.685 --> 00:01:40.414
how far their midpoint sits from origin.

00:01:40.494 --> 00:01:44.487
Its saturating sigmoid forces a trade-off between the two.

00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:48.981
Midpoint-Displacement makes this explicit with one
knob, lambda,

00:01:49.061 --> 00:01:51.720
reproducing CCS or maximizing accuracy.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:59.743
Experiments use hidden-state activations from four
models: the encoder and decoder of UnifiedQA T5-Large,

00:01:59.823 --> 00:02:04.597
DeBERTa, and GPT-Neo, averaged over five datasets
including BoolQ.

00:02:04.677 --> 00:02:07.833
Contrast pairs append two exclusive answers,

00:02:07.913 --> 00:02:13.570
each set normalized independently so the probe
cannot detect the answer token.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:20.057
The headline: Midpoint-Displacement tuned to
imitate CCS gives probe directions averaging

00:02:20.137 --> 00:02:24.506
cosine similarity about zero point six three to
real CCS probes,

00:02:24.586 --> 00:02:28.676
while CCS agrees with itself only at zero point
seven eight.

00:02:28.756 --> 00:02:32.847
Other accurate losses like PCA sit near zero point
one five,

00:02:32.927 --> 00:02:36.740
so the resemblance is specific to Midpoint-Displacement.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.506
The two Midpoint-Displacement variants differ only
in lambda.

00:02:41.586 --> 00:02:46.485
Tuned to mimic CCS, cosine similarity is about zero
point six three;

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:50.512
tuned for accuracy, it drops to zero point three eight,

00:02:50.592 --> 00:02:55.784
showing the displacement-versus-midpoint trade-off
defines CCS's target.

00:02:55.864 --> 00:03:00.250
Retuning lambda raises test accuracy to zero point
seven six.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:08.500
A few numbers: Midpoint-Displacement matched to CCS
reaches average cosine similarity about zero point six three,

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.018
versus CCS self-similarity zero point seven eight,

00:03:12.098 --> 00:03:15.817
while competing losses reach only zero point one five.

00:03:15.897 --> 00:03:22.431
The accuracy-tuned variant averages zero point
seven six accuracy versus zero point seven one,

00:03:22.511 --> 00:03:26.230
winning on three of four models, across five datasets.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:33.251
The take-home: CCS succeeds because of the
displacement information in its contrast-pair data,

00:03:33.331 --> 00:03:35.396
not its specific loss formula.

00:03:35.476 --> 00:03:40.687
A simple Midpoint-Displacement loss reproduces CCS
and, retuned, beats it.

00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:43.118
The exact loss is interchangeable;

00:03:43.198 --> 00:03:47.480
the real lever is the displacement-versus-midpoint
trade-off.
